The correlation between oil and long bonds ($USO - $TLT) has hit an all-time inverted extreme. Up until now, it would have been reasonable to expect higher oil = higher yields.

But that trade may have just run out of steam, as this series is highly mean reverting.

A breakdown in correlation between oil and bonds can play out in one of two ways:

- Oil moves lower but yields remain stubbornly high; this is the bullish setup, as it argues yields are high because growth is strong (arguable, but that’s a different discussion);

- Oil rises, but yields move lower; this is the bearish scenario, as it suggests growth is contracting due to higher oil prices;

The way this plays out is the key to the path forward for equities.

